#!/usr/bin/env python3
r"""Penyuntingan bahasa tahap 1 untuk manuscript/sn-article.tex: 20 suntingan (Abstract, Introduction, Discussion, Limitations, Conclusion, Results 4.3 dan 4.4).
Tujuan: kalimat lebih pendek dan langsung, tanpa pola kontras berulang ('not merely', 'rather than', 'no longer X -- it is Y'), tanpa rantai titik koma, dan tanpa pengulangan angka yang sama.
Setiap suntingan hanya mengganti teks lama yang COCOK PERSIS dan muncul TEPAT SEKALI. Kutipan (\cite), rujukan silang (\ref, \label), rumus, dan semua angka sudah diperiksa identik
antara teks lama dan baru (kecuali dua angka yang sengaja tidak diulang di LM1). Suntingan yang teks lamanya tidak cocok (berkas berubah sejak analisis) DILEWATI dan dilaporkan;
suntingan yang cocok lebih dari sekali membatalkan seluruh proses. Idempoten. Akhir baris dipertahankan. Cadangan: .pre-prose1.bak.
   python scripts/apply_prose_edits_1.py [--tex manuscript/sn-article.tex] [--dry-run]"""
import argparse, shutil, sys
from pathlib import Path
EDITS = [
  ('AB1', 'Abstract',
   'Post hoc analyses qualify these gains: exact Shapley attribution puts 78.9\\% on exploitability and the KEV indicator, ingredients of the label; without them HANFIS has no skill over CVSS (skill $-0.14$, interval $-0.34$ to $0.05$); and a four-parameter formula of those two inputs, $100\\max(x_2,0.1\\,x_7)$, has a lower error than HANFIS at a four-week horizon (skill $-0.53$, interval $-1.44$ to $-0.21$); the gap narrows at longer horizons only through weeks around an EPSS model change.',
   'Post hoc analyses qualify these gains. Exact Shapley attribution puts 78.9\\% on exploitability and the KEV indicator, ingredients of the label, and without them HANFIS has no skill over CVSS (skill $-0.14$, interval $-0.34$ to $0.05$). A four-parameter formula of those two inputs, $100\\max(x_2,0.1\\,x_7)$, has a lower error than HANFIS at a four-week horizon (skill $-0.53$, interval $-1.44$ to $-0.21$); the gap narrows at longer horizons only through weeks around an EPSS model change.'),
  ('AB2', 'Abstract',
   'HANFIS is therefore a compact rule base that does not demonstrably exceed, and at the four-week horizon falls behind, a simple formula, and the data cannot show whether environmental context improves forecasts.',
   'At the four-week horizon HANFIS therefore falls behind a simple formula, and at longer horizons it does not demonstrably exceed it. It remains a compact rule base, and the data cannot show whether environmental context improves forecasts.'),
  ('IN1', 'Introduction',
   'These characteristics improve elasticity and operational agility, but they also reduce the useful lifetime of security assessments derived from periodic observations.',
   'These characteristics improve elasticity and operational agility, but they shorten the useful life of security assessments derived from periodic observations.'),
  ('IN2', 'Introduction',
   'Consequently, cloud-native risk assessment must become sufficiently adaptive to follow changes in vulnerability condition, workload exposure, authentication posture, asset importance, and external threat activity.',
   'Cloud-native risk assessment therefore has to follow changes in vulnerability status, workload exposure, authentication posture, asset importance, and external threat activity.'),
  ('IN3', 'Introduction',
   'The resulting research gap is not simply a conflict between subjective and objective assessment. It is the absence of a reproducible mechanism that preserves expert interpretability while adapting the input--output relationship from observed data and continuously recalculating risk from the current system state.',
   'What is missing is a reproducible mechanism that preserves expert interpretability while adapting the input--output relationship from observed data and continuously recalculating risk from the current system state.'),
  ('IN4', 'Introduction',
   'This study, therefore, proposes a domain-informed Hierarchical Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference System (HANFIS).',
   'This study proposes a domain-informed Hierarchical Adaptive Neuro-Fuzzy Inference System (HANFIS).'),
  ('IN5', 'Introduction',
   'The hierarchical decomposition is not merely a conceptual rearrangement.',
   'The hierarchical decomposition also changes the rule count directly.'),
  ('IN6', 'Introduction',
   'Accordingly, computational optimization is treated as a measurable research objective rather than an assumed benefit.',
   'The computational benefit is therefore treated as something to measure rather than assume.'),
  ('IN7', 'Introduction',
   'Together, these contributions position HANFIS as more than a higher-capacity replacement for static fuzzy assessment. The proposed model is intended to provide a traceable bridge from expert knowledge to data-adaptive inference, from high-dimensional risk factors to a computationally tractable rule base, and, as designed but not yet evaluated extensions, from risk estimation to bounded operational response and to edge execution.',
   'Together, these contributions aim to connect expert knowledge to data-adaptive inference, and seven risk inputs to a computationally tractable rule base. Bounded operational response and edge execution are designed extensions that this paper does not evaluate.'),
  ('DS1', 'Discussion',
   "Three findings from the completed experiments (E1, E3, and the observability-overhead runs) shape how\nthe confirmatory comparison in Section~\\ref{subssec4.3} should be read. First, the\nlabel is dominated by its own recent history: a 4-week persistence forecast recovers 96\\% of the\nclimatology-relative skill on the pilot inventory, so any claim that HANFIS ``predicts risk'' must be a\nclaim about improving on a strong autoregressive baseline, not about beating an uninformative one --\nthis is why H1 was reframed against persistence rather than against Fuzzyfortify or CVSS alone\n(Section~\\ref{sec1}). Second, a single CVSS score carries essentially no explanatory power for the\nrealised 30-day exploitability exposure ($\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{clim}}\\approx0$), which is itself\nevidence for the research gap C3 claims to address: severity and realised exploitation are different\nquantities, and conflating them is a plausible source of alert fatigue in practice. Third, the E1\nablation shows that a monotonicity constraint intended to keep the model auditable (higher risk factors\nmust not lower the score) has a real, family-dependent accuracy cost on data containing thresholds; the\nchoice of Gaussian membership functions with monotonicity active is therefore a deliberate trade of some accuracy for a response that, for the dominant risk factors, rarely falls as they rise (Section~\\ref{subsec:e8}), not a default that happened to be convenient.",
   "Three findings from the completed experiments (E1, E3, and the observability-overhead runs) shape how the confirmatory comparison in Section~\\ref{subssec4.3} should be read. First, the label is dominated by its own recent history. A 4-week persistence forecast recovers 96\\% of the climatology-relative skill on the pilot inventory, so any claim that HANFIS ``predicts risk'' is a claim about improving on a strong autoregressive baseline, not about beating an uninformative one. This is why H1 was reframed against persistence rather than against Fuzzyfortify or CVSS alone (Section~\\ref{sec1}). Second, a single CVSS score carries essentially no explanatory power for the realised 30-day exploitability exposure ($\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{clim}}\\approx0$). Severity and realised exploitation are different quantities, and conflating them is a plausible source of alert fatigue in practice; this is itself evidence for the research gap that C3 claims to address. Third, the E1 ablation shows that a monotonicity constraint intended to keep the model auditable (higher risk factors must not lower the score) has a real, family-dependent accuracy cost on data containing thresholds. Choosing Gaussian membership functions with monotonicity active therefore trades some accuracy for a response that, for the dominant risk factors, rarely falls as they rise (Section~\\ref{subsec:e8}); it was a deliberate choice, not a default that happened to be convenient."),
  ('DS2', 'Discussion',
   "The post hoc analyses of Sections~\\ref{subsec:e8} and~\\ref{subsec:e9} qualify how far these findings generalise. Exact Shapley attribution places 78.9\\% of the model's attribution on exploitability and the KEV indicator, which are ingredients of the realised label, and only 3.3\\% on the three environmental inputs; without those two inputs the model shows no demonstrable skill over a single CVSS score, and it is not demonstrably better than gradient boosting restricted to them. What the data support is therefore narrower: a 34-rule hierarchical model estimates near-term exploitability exposure from current EPSS and KEV signals about as accurately as a persistence forecast that needs past labels (the two are not distinguishable), and more accurately than generic linear and boosted models, but not more accurately than a four-parameter formula of the same two signals (Section~\\ref{subsec:e9}), which, together with the interaction ridge, has the lowest error of all predictors we compared at the four-week horizon (at eight and twelve weeks the gap to the formula narrows, but only through weeks around the EPSS model change, Sections~\\ref{subsec:e14} and~\\ref{subsec:e16}); the ranking of assets by predicted risk is almost the same for all predictors at four weeks (Section~\\ref{subsec:e15}); the data do not support the claim that the vulnerability--environment decomposition, or the environmental inputs, improve prediction. Because the target is built from EPSS, KEV and reachability alone and does not depend on the three environmental inputs, this inventory is not designed to show a benefit of those inputs even if one exists; testing it requires an outcome that depends on the environment, such as incident or impact data. A forward evaluation on weeks after the protocol was frozen is pre-registered (Section~\\ref{subsec:e7}) and will be run once its labels have matured, on 2 November 2026.",
   "The post hoc analyses of Sections~\\ref{subsec:e8} and~\\ref{subsec:e9} limit how far these findings generalise. Exact Shapley attribution places 78.9\\% of the model's attribution on exploitability and the KEV indicator, which are ingredients of the realised label, and only 3.3\\% on the three environmental inputs. Without those two inputs the model shows no demonstrable skill over a single CVSS score, and it is not demonstrably better than gradient boosting restricted to them. What the data support is therefore narrower. A 34-rule hierarchical model estimates near-term exploitability exposure from current EPSS and KEV signals about as accurately as a persistence forecast that needs past labels (the two are not distinguishable), and more accurately than generic linear and boosted models. It is not more accurate than a four-parameter formula of the same two signals (Section~\\ref{subsec:e9}); this formula, together with the interaction ridge, has the lowest error of all predictors we compared at the four-week horizon. At eight and twelve weeks the gap to the formula narrows, but only through weeks around the EPSS model change (Sections~\\ref{subsec:e14} and~\\ref{subsec:e16}). The ranking of assets by predicted risk is almost the same for all predictors at four weeks (Section~\\ref{subsec:e15}). The data do not support the claim that the vulnerability--environment decomposition, or the environmental inputs, improve prediction. Because the target is built from EPSS, KEV and reachability alone and does not depend on the three environmental inputs, this inventory is not designed to show a benefit of those inputs even if one exists; testing that benefit requires an outcome that depends on the environment, such as incident or impact data. A forward evaluation on weeks after the protocol was frozen is pre-registered (Section~\\ref{subsec:e7}) and will be run on 2 November 2026, once its labels have matured."),
  ('DS3', 'Discussion',
   "The instrumentation-overhead results (Section~\\ref{subsec4.1}) support a second, narrower claim:\nthat Kubernetes observability telemetry can be treated as a fixed resource cost rather than folded into\nthe risk model as a confound. Node-level CPU increases by 99.4~mCPU under monitoring while the\nmonitoring namespace itself accounts for only 39.8~mCPU, so pod-level accounting alone understates the\ntrue platform cost by a factor of approximately 2.5; despite this, workload latency shows no detectable\nchange (95\\% confidence intervals within $\\pm 0.27$~ms). This separates the cost of observing the system\nfrom any change in the thing being observed, which is the premise the environmental subsystem's Kubernetes-exposure input relies on; whether that input improves prediction is a separate question, and on the present inventory the environmental inputs add no demonstrable predictive information (Section~\\ref{subsec:e9}).",
   'The instrumentation-overhead results (Section~\\ref{subsec4.1}) support a second, narrower claim: Kubernetes observability telemetry can be treated as a fixed resource cost rather than folded into the risk model as a confound. Node-level CPU increases by 99.4~mCPU under monitoring, while the monitoring namespace itself accounts for only 39.8~mCPU, so pod-level accounting alone understates the platform cost by a factor of about 2.5. Workload latency nonetheless shows no detectable change (95\\% confidence intervals within $\\pm 0.27$~ms). The cost of observing the system is thus separated from any change in the system being observed, which is the premise behind the Kubernetes-exposure input of the environmental subsystem. Whether that input improves prediction is a separate question, and on the present inventory the environmental inputs add no demonstrable predictive information (Section~\\ref{subsec:e9}).'),
  ('LM1', 'Limitations',
   "Two limits of construct validity deserve emphasis before the usual caveats. First, the label and two of the inputs share ingredients: $x_2$ (EPSS $\\times\\,\\rho$) and $x_7$ (the KEV indicator) are the values at $t$ of quantities that the label aggregates over $t{+}7,\\dots,t{+}28$, and runtime reachability $\\rho$ enters both. The task is therefore to forecast the near future of exploitability signals that the model can already see, not to predict independent compromise outcomes, for which no ground truth exists here; the persistence baseline, built in the same way, is the relevant bar, and the comparison with a single CVSS score should be read in that light; exact Shapley attribution (Section~\\ref{subsec:e8}) places 78.9\\% of the model's attribution on these two inputs and 3.3\\% on the environment inputs, and without these two inputs the model shows no demonstrable skill over a single CVSS score (Section~\\ref{subsec:e9}). Second, the dynamic character of the score comes from the time-varying EPSS and KEV feeds and from runtime reachability; runtime telemetry (CPU, memory, latency) is used only to quantify observability overhead and is not a model input, so ``dynamic'' must not be read as telemetry-driven scoring. The rule-level attribution is faithful on average but not uniformly (Section~\\ref{sec:e6}), and we do not claim validated explanations.",
   "Two limits of construct validity come first. First, the label and two of the inputs share ingredients: $x_2$ (EPSS $\\times\\,\\rho$) and $x_7$ (the KEV indicator) are the values at $t$ of quantities that the label aggregates over $t{+}7,\\dots,t{+}28$, and runtime reachability $\\rho$ enters both. The task is therefore to forecast the near future of exploitability signals that the model can already see, not to predict independent compromise outcomes, for which no ground truth exists here. The persistence baseline, built in the same way, is the relevant bar, and the comparison with a single CVSS score should be read in that light. The exact Shapley attribution (Section~\\ref{subsec:e8}) and the ablation without these two inputs (Section~\\ref{subsec:e9}) show how heavily the model's skill rests on them. Second, the dynamic character of the score comes from the time-varying EPSS and KEV feeds and from runtime reachability. Runtime telemetry (CPU, memory, latency) is used only to quantify observability overhead and is not a model input, so ``dynamic'' must not be read as telemetry-driven scoring. The rule-level attribution is faithful on average but not uniformly (Section~\\ref{sec:e6}), and we do not claim validated explanations."),
  ('LM2', 'Limitations',
   "The confirmatory comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results}) uses the full 120-asset inventory. On the same inventory (6{,}357 labelled rows; Table~\\ref{tab:e3-baselines}), a linear ridge model over all seven inputs underperforms persistence on every one of the five grouped-temporal folds ($\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}=-2.293$ pooled, raw scale); we suspect that the linear model over-extrapolates as the inventory's heterogeneity grows, where persistence's week-to-week copy remains comparatively safe, but we did not test this explanation. The EPSS scoring model changed version (\\texttt{v2025.03.14} to\n\\texttt{v2026.06.15}) inside the sealed 57-week window, a threat to label comparability across that\nboundary; two of the five held-out blocks involve labels computed under the newer version, and a post hoc\nre-analysis restricted to weeks whose labels all pre-date the change leaves the confirmatory conclusions\nunchanged (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}). The data-sufficiency gate (at least ten observations per parameter) is met by the pooled\nsample ($6{,}357$ labelled rows against 176 parameters) but not by the two earliest held-out folds ($2.2$ and $7.1$ observations per\nparameter), which are also those in which HANFIS is furthest behind persistence, and H2's pooled margin is dominated by the\nfirst fold (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}). CVEs lacking a Trivy-reported publication date are\ndated by their first appearance in a sealed EPSS snapshot, which is conservative (at most 7 days late) but not exact. A single CVE whose Trivy status lies outside the accepted set (for example ``under investigation'' for a freshly published Red Hat advisory) makes the whole asset-week unusable; this affected one of the 120 images for seven consecutive weeks from 2026-08-10, can reach at most the two Red Hat based images of the inventory, and removes such rows from the analysis. Labels whose four-week window straddles the EPSS model change of 2026-06-15 (and the persistence labels derived from them) mix two score definitions; these rows (18\\% of the out-of-fold rows) hold 77\\% of the squared error of persistence and of the formula, and the forward evaluation avoids them. The label aggregation rule (a row-wise maximum over active CVEs), its numeric scale, and\nthe operational definition of Patch Risk were closed and pre-registered as amendment A-13\n(cross-checked against the literal target-definition formula, Section~\\ref{subsec:target-definition})\nbefore the confirmatory comparison in Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results} was run. The testbed is a\nsingle two-node WSL~2/Docker Desktop Kubernetes cluster; external validity to production multi-node\nclusters and other CNI/storage stacks is untested. Ten paired observability runs, while showing large\nand consistent effects, still provide limited power for null-hypothesis testing on the latency measure\nspecifically, which is why the reported confidence intervals rather than a significance threshold carry\nthe interpretive weight in Section~\\ref{subsec4.1}.",
   "The confirmatory comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results}) uses the full 120-asset inventory. On the same inventory (6{,}357 labelled rows; Table~\\ref{tab:e3-baselines}), a linear ridge model over all seven inputs underperforms persistence on every one of the five grouped-temporal folds ($\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}=-2.293$ pooled, raw scale); we suspect that the linear model over-extrapolates as the inventory's heterogeneity grows, where persistence's week-to-week copy remains comparatively safe, but we did not test this explanation.\n\nThe EPSS scoring model changed version (\\texttt{v2025.03.14} to\n\\texttt{v2026.06.15}) inside the sealed 57-week window, a threat to label comparability across that\nboundary; two of the five held-out blocks involve labels computed under the newer version, and a post hoc\nre-analysis restricted to weeks whose labels all pre-date the change leaves the confirmatory conclusions\nunchanged (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}).\n\nThe data-sufficiency gate (at least ten observations per parameter) is met by the pooled\nsample ($6{,}357$ labelled rows against 176 parameters) but not by the two earliest held-out folds ($2.2$ and $7.1$ observations per\nparameter), which are also those in which HANFIS is furthest behind persistence, and H2's pooled margin is dominated by the\nfirst fold (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}).\n\nCVEs lacking a Trivy-reported publication date are\ndated by their first appearance in a sealed EPSS snapshot, which is conservative (at most 7 days late) but not exact. A single CVE whose Trivy status lies outside the accepted set (for example ``under investigation'' for a freshly published Red Hat advisory) makes the whole asset-week unusable; this affected one of the 120 images for seven consecutive weeks from 2026-08-10, can reach at most the two Red Hat based images of the inventory, and removes such rows from the analysis.\n\nLabels whose four-week window straddles the EPSS model change of 2026-06-15 (and the persistence labels derived from them) mix two score definitions; these rows (18\\% of the out-of-fold rows) hold 77\\% of the squared error of persistence and of the formula, and the forward evaluation avoids them.\n\nThe label aggregation rule (a row-wise maximum over active CVEs), its numeric scale, and\nthe operational definition of Patch Risk were closed and pre-registered as amendment A-13\n(cross-checked against the literal target-definition formula, Section~\\ref{subsec:target-definition})\nbefore the confirmatory comparison in Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results} was run.\n\nThe testbed is a\nsingle two-node WSL~2/Docker Desktop Kubernetes cluster; external validity to production multi-node\nclusters and other CNI/storage stacks is untested. Ten paired observability runs, while showing large\nand consistent effects, still provide limited power for null-hypothesis testing on the latency measure\nspecifically, which is why the reported confidence intervals rather than a significance threshold carry\nthe interpretive weight in Section~\\ref{subsec4.1}."),
  ('C1', 'Conclusion',
   "This paper proposes HANFIS, a domain-informed hierarchical ANFIS that reduces a 128-rule, 1{,}052-parameter\nflat Sugeno system over seven cloud-native risk inputs to 34 rules and 176 parameters\n(Section~\\ref{subsec:hanfis-architecture}) by separating vulnerability and environmental reasoning into\ntwo first-tier panels before an integration tier combines them with dynamic threat intelligence. Under a\nprotocol frozen and dated before training began (Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results}), HANFIS confirms a\nsubstantial reduction in estimation error against a single CVSS score (H1a), a linear model over the same\nseven inputs (H1c), and a parameter-matched flat ANFIS initialised by subtractive clustering (H2); it is\nnot yet distinguishable from a causal 4-week persistence forecast (H1b), which this study's own mandatory\nbaselines (Section~\\ref{subssec4.3}) show to be a far harder bar than static expert scoring, not an\nafterthought comparator. H2 is confirmed as non-inferiority to a capacity-matched flat model, and HANFIS has the lower error in four of the\nfive held-out blocks (Section~\\ref{subssec4.3}); the pooled margin is, however, dominated by the first block, where\ntraining is thinnest, so it is read as suggestive of, not as evidence for, a structural advantage of the\nvulnerability/environmental/threat decomposition (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}). Post hoc analyses qualify this further: the model's skill rests on exploitability and KEV (78.9\\% of the exact Shapley attribution; without them no skill over CVSS) and a four-parameter formula of those two inputs has a lower error than HANFIS (Sections~\\ref{subsec:e8} and~\\ref{subsec:e9}), so on this target HANFIS offers a compact rule base with a documented response surface rather than better accuracy than a simple formula at the four-week horizon (at eight and twelve weeks the gap narrows but does not close on rows unaffected by the EPSS model change, and the other inputs do not contribute; Sections~\\ref{subsec:e14} and~\\ref{subsec:e16}), and whether environmental context improves forecasts remains open. Two further contributions are established: the real-world data pipeline itself (sealed,\nhash-verified EPSS and CISA KEV evidence feeding a gated inventory of 120 assets and 6{,}357 labelled\nasset-weeks, Section~\\ref{subsec:target-definition}), and a completed ablation showing that the\nmonotonicity constraint intended to keep the score from falling as risk factors rise (met in practice only for the dominant drivers; Section~\\ref{subsec:e8}) has a real,\nmembership-family-dependent accuracy cost (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}).",
   "This paper proposes HANFIS, a domain-informed hierarchical ANFIS. It reduces a 128-rule, 1{,}052-parameter flat Sugeno system over seven cloud-native risk inputs to 34 rules and 176 parameters (Section~\\ref{subsec:hanfis-architecture}) by separating vulnerability and environmental reasoning into two first-tier panels before an integration tier combines them with dynamic threat intelligence. Under a protocol frozen and dated before training began (Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results}), HANFIS confirms a substantial reduction in estimation error against a single CVSS score (H1a), a linear model over the same seven inputs (H1c), and a parameter-matched flat ANFIS initialised by subtractive clustering (H2). It is not yet distinguishable from a causal 4-week persistence forecast (H1b), which the study's own mandatory baselines (Section~\\ref{subssec4.3}) show to be a far harder bar than static expert scoring. H2 is confirmed as non-inferiority to a capacity-matched flat model, and HANFIS has the lower error in four of the five held-out blocks (Section~\\ref{subssec4.3}). The pooled margin is, however, dominated by the first block, where training is thinnest, so it is read as suggestive of a structural advantage of the vulnerability/environmental/threat decomposition, not as evidence for one (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5}).\n\nPost hoc analyses qualify this further. The model's skill rests on exploitability and KEV (78.9\\% of the exact Shapley attribution; without them it has no skill over CVSS), and a four-parameter formula of those two inputs has a lower error than HANFIS (Sections~\\ref{subsec:e8} and~\\ref{subsec:e9}). On this target HANFIS therefore offers a compact rule base with a documented response surface, not better accuracy than a simple formula at the four-week horizon. At eight and twelve weeks the gap narrows but does not close on rows unaffected by the EPSS model change, and the other inputs do not contribute (Sections~\\ref{subsec:e14} and~\\ref{subsec:e16}). Whether environmental context improves forecasts remains open. Two further contributions are established: the real-world data pipeline itself (sealed, hash-verified EPSS and CISA KEV evidence feeding a gated inventory of 120 assets and 6{,}357 labelled asset-weeks, Section~\\ref{subsec:target-definition}), and a completed ablation showing that the monotonicity constraint intended to keep the score from falling as risk factors rise (met in practice only for the dominant drivers; Section~\\ref{subsec:e8}) has a real, membership-family-dependent accuracy cost (Section~\\ref{subssec4.5})."),
  ('C2', 'Conclusion',
   "Confirming H1b against persistence is the main open question; the bottleneck-width sweep\n(Section~\\ref{subssec4.4}) found that widening the scalar inter-tier bottleneck from $k{=}1$ to $k{=}2$\ndoes not close the gap to persistence and is significantly worse than it. Re-scanning every image with\na single frozen vulnerability database, so that no image's CVE surface differs merely because it was\nscanned on a different day, remains an open methodological item but is not expected to change the\nqualitative picture given the gate margin already achieved. The auditable policy-translation layer\n(contribution C4) and edge deployment (originally hypothesis H5, now future work since the present\ntestbed runs HANFIS cluster-side rather than on its edge node) remain designed but unevaluated, and are\nleft for a subsequent study once the predictive\ncomparison above is closed.",
   "Confirming H1b against persistence is the main open question. The bottleneck-width sweep (Section~\\ref{subssec4.4}) found that widening the scalar inter-tier bottleneck from $k{=}1$ to $k{=}2$ does not close the gap to persistence; the wider model is significantly worse than persistence. Re-scanning every image with a single frozen vulnerability database, so that no image's CVE surface differs merely because it was scanned on a different day, remains an open methodological item, but given the gate margin already achieved it is not expected to change the qualitative picture. The auditable policy-translation layer (contribution C4) and edge deployment (originally hypothesis H5, now future work because the present testbed runs HANFIS cluster-side rather than on its edge node) remain designed but unevaluated. Both are left for a subsequent study once the predictive comparison above is closed."),
  ('R1', 'Results (4.3)',
   "H1b's confidence interval crosses zero, so HANFIS is not yet distinguishable from a causal 4-week\npersistence forecast -- the same pattern observed in the E3 pilot (Section~\\ref{subssec4.3}'s baseline\ntable above), and the honest reading is that the strongly autocorrelated label remains the hardest bar\nin this study. The post hoc analysis in Section~\\ref{subssec4.5} indicates why: HANFIS outperforms persistence\nwhen the label moves and underperforms it when the label is stable. H2's margin is large for two models holding an\nidentical 176-parameter budget, though not uniform across folds: HANFIS wins on four of the five\nheld-out blocks, with per-fold skill against FlatANFISMatched ranging from 0.165 to 0.924; the fifth\nfold (fold 1) is negative ($\\mathrm{SS}=-0.122$) and coincides with a secondary diagnostic in which\nHANFIS's predicted variance exceeds the label's own variance by more than three orders of magnitude\non several near-constant-label images in that fold (ratio >$1{,}000\\times$ in the most extreme case),\nconsistent with the model under-damping day-to-day EPSS/CVSS snapshot noise where the weekly\naggregate carries little real signal -- a limitation discussed further in Section~\\ref{subsec5.1}, not\na single outlier fold driving an otherwise uniform result. FlatANFISMatched's held-out predictions\nnonetheless range well outside the plausible label interval (down to $-6.679$ and up to $11.059$,\nagainst HANFIS's $0.000$--$10.401$) in a manner consistent with poor extrapolation from\nunsupervised cluster centres that have no access to the label's own generative grouping -- precisely\nthe vulnerability/environmental/threat decomposition HANFIS's architecture encodes by construction\n(Section~\\ref{subsec:hanfis-architecture}). This is read as evidence for the mechanism claimed in\nC1/H2 rather than a methodological artefact: the random 15\\% validation carve-out used only to select\nthe best of three restarts is identical for both models, while the reported skill score itself comes\nfrom the temporally and group-disjoint held-out block that carve-out never touches.",
   "H1b's confidence interval crosses zero, so HANFIS is not yet distinguishable from a causal 4-week persistence forecast. The E3 pilot showed the same pattern (Section~\\ref{subssec4.3}'s baseline table above): the strongly autocorrelated label remains the hardest bar in this study. The post hoc analysis in Section~\\ref{subssec4.5} indicates why: HANFIS outperforms persistence when the label moves and underperforms it when the label is stable.\n\nH2's margin is large for two models holding an identical 176-parameter budget, but it is not uniform across folds. HANFIS wins on four of the five held-out blocks, with per-fold skill against FlatANFISMatched ranging from 0.165 to 0.924. The fifth fold (fold 1) is negative ($\\mathrm{SS}=-0.122$). It coincides with a secondary diagnostic in which HANFIS's predicted variance exceeds the label's own variance by more than three orders of magnitude on several near-constant-label images in that fold (ratio >$1{,}000\\times$ in the most extreme case). This is consistent with the model under-damping day-to-day EPSS/CVSS snapshot noise where the weekly aggregate carries little real signal, a limitation discussed further in Section~\\ref{subsec5.1}; it is not a single outlier fold driving an otherwise uniform result.\n\nFlatANFISMatched's held-out predictions nonetheless range well outside the plausible label interval (down to $-6.679$ and up to $11.059$, against HANFIS's $0.000$--$10.401$). This is consistent with poor extrapolation from unsupervised cluster centres that have no access to the label's own generative grouping, which is the vulnerability/environmental/threat decomposition that HANFIS's architecture encodes by construction (Section~\\ref{subsec:hanfis-architecture}). We read this as evidence for the mechanism claimed in C1/H2, not as a methodological artefact: the random 15\\% validation carve-out used only to select the best of three restarts is identical for both models, while the reported skill score itself comes from the temporally and group-disjoint held-out block that the carve-out never touches."),
  ('R2', 'Results (4.4)',
   'The scalar inter-tier bottleneck between the lower panels and the integration panel is a candidate\nexplanation for any residual gap between HANFIS and a full-capacity flat ANFIS: compressing $v$ and $e$\nto a single number may discard cross-subsystem interaction that the flat model can still represent. A\nsweep over the bottleneck width $k$ -- widening $v$ and $e$ from scalars to $k$-dimensional vectors\npassed into the integration panel -- traces this trade-off directly, but grid-partitioning the\nintegration tier makes its rule count grow as $m_I^{2k}\\cdot m$: at the frozen $m_I=3$, $k=1$ reproduces\nHANFIS exactly (34 rules, 176 parameters), $k=2$ already costs 178 rules and 1{,}152 parameters (6.5x),\nand $k\\ge3$ exceeds 1{,}400 rules (Figure~\\ref{fig:e5-rule-growth}) -- more parameters than the present\ninventory has rows, and the sweep script refuses these by default rather than attempting an\nunderdetermined fit. $k\\in\\{1,2\\}$ were run; $k=4$, originally planned, was dropped on these grounds and\nthis combinatorial explosion is itself read as evidence for why a scalar bottleneck was the design\nchoice in the first place, independent of what $k=1$ vs.\\ $k=2$ shows empirically.',
   'The scalar inter-tier bottleneck between the lower panels and the integration panel is a candidate explanation for any residual gap between HANFIS and a full-capacity flat ANFIS: compressing $v$ and $e$ to a single number may discard cross-subsystem interaction that the flat model can still represent. A sweep over the bottleneck width $k$, widening $v$ and $e$ from scalars to $k$-dimensional vectors passed into the integration panel, traces this trade-off directly. Grid-partitioning the integration tier, however, makes its rule count grow as $m_I^{2k}\\cdot m$. At the frozen $m_I=3$, $k=1$ reproduces HANFIS exactly (34 rules, 176 parameters), $k=2$ already costs 178 rules and 1{,}152 parameters (6.5x), and $k\\ge3$ exceeds 1{,}400 rules (Figure~\\ref{fig:e5-rule-growth}), which is more parameters than the present inventory has rows. The sweep script refuses these settings by default rather than attempting an underdetermined fit. We ran $k\\in\\{1,2\\}$; $k=4$, originally planned, was dropped on these grounds. This combinatorial explosion is itself read as evidence for why a scalar bottleneck was the design choice in the first place, independent of what $k=1$ vs.\\ $k=2$ shows empirically.'),
  ('R3', 'Results (4.4)',
   "$k{=}1$ recovers skill broadly consistent with HANFIS's own H1b result\n(Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results}: $\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}=0.015$, $[-0.607,0.246]$) -- both\nconfidence intervals cross zero and overlap substantially, as the per-row parity above predicts,\nthough the point estimates ($0.169$ vs.\\ $0.015$) agree less closely than on the smaller inventory.\n$k{=}2$'s interval now excludes zero entirely on the negative side: on the completed 120-asset\ninventory, widening the bottleneck is no longer merely unsupported by evidence -- it is\nsignificantly worse than persistence. The paired comparison on matched resampled images -- the same\nresample used for both arms, so sampling noise is shared -- gives\n$\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}(k{=}1)-\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}(k{=}2)=0.791$, 95\\% CI\n$[0.354,2.037]$, excluding zero (100\\% of resamples favour $k{=}1$); $k{=}1$ is pooled-RMSE-better\non all five folds (two-sided sign test $p=0.0625$, the floor attainable with five folds) -- the same\nmargin of evidence as on the smaller inventory, even as each arm's marginal significance against\npersistence flipped in opposite directions.",
   "$k{=}1$ recovers skill broadly consistent with HANFIS's own H1b result (Table~\\ref{tab:e4-results}: $\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}=0.015$, $[-0.607,0.246]$). Both confidence intervals cross zero and overlap substantially, as the per-row parity above predicts, though the point estimates ($0.169$ vs.\\ $0.015$) agree less closely than on the smaller inventory. $k{=}2$'s interval now excludes zero entirely on the negative side: on the completed 120-asset inventory, widening the bottleneck is significantly worse than persistence, whereas on the smaller inventory it had been unsupported by the evidence but not shown to be worse. The paired comparison on matched resampled images (the same resample used for both arms, so sampling noise is shared) gives $\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}(k{=}1)-\\mathrm{SS}_{\\mathrm{pers}}(k{=}2)=0.791$, 95\\% CI $[0.354,2.037]$, excluding zero (100\\% of resamples favour $k{=}1$). $k{=}1$ is pooled-RMSE-better on all five folds (two-sided sign test $p=0.0625$, the floor attainable with five folds), the same margin of evidence as on the smaller inventory, even as each arm's marginal significance against persistence flipped in opposite directions."),
  ('R4', 'Results (4.4)',
   "This is the strongest statement the present inventory supports, and it remains a mechanism claim\nrather than a claim that wider bottlenecks are intrinsically worse: every $k{=}2$ fold falls under ten\nobservations per parameter (range 0.3--2.9), the same ten-per-parameter threshold applied elsewhere in\nthis study (Section~\\ref{subsec:target-definition}), while $k{=}1$ clears it in three of five folds. A\nsecondary diagnostic separates part of this gap from pure data scarcity: disabling the hard clamp on\n$v,e$ (an ablation, not the model HANFIS reports) costs $k{=}1$ 0.161 of skill but costs $k{=}2$\n0.813 (both re-run on the completed 120-asset inventory; up from 0.019 and 0.394 respectively\non the 27-image pilot) -- consistent with $v,e$ being estimated less reliably from the same training rows once six\ntimes as many consequent parameters compete for them, so that excursions outside $[0,1]$ are both\nmore frequent and more consequential once clamped. Separating an intrinsic cost of wider bottlenecks\nfrom this data-scale effect requires an inventory substantially larger than the 120 assets collected\nhere, left for future work; the architecture's own combinatorial growth in $k$\n(Figure~\\ref{fig:e5-rule-growth}) means that separation gets harder, not easier, as $k$ increases.",
   "This is the strongest statement the present inventory supports. It is a mechanism claim, not a claim that wider bottlenecks are intrinsically worse: every $k{=}2$ fold falls under ten observations per parameter (range 0.3--2.9), the same ten-per-parameter threshold applied elsewhere in this study (Section~\\ref{subsec:target-definition}), while $k{=}1$ clears it in three of five folds. A secondary diagnostic separates part of this gap from pure data scarcity: disabling the hard clamp on $v,e$ (an ablation, not the model HANFIS reports) costs $k{=}1$ 0.161 of skill but costs $k{=}2$ 0.813 (both re-run on the completed 120-asset inventory; up from 0.019 and 0.394 respectively on the 27-image pilot). This is consistent with $v,e$ being estimated less reliably from the same training rows once six times as many consequent parameters compete for them, so that excursions outside $[0,1]$ are both more frequent and more consequential once clamped. Separating an intrinsic cost of wider bottlenecks from this data-scale effect requires an inventory substantially larger than the 120 assets collected here, which is left for future work. The architecture's own combinatorial growth in $k$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:e5-rule-growth}) means that this separation gets harder, not easier, as $k$ increases."),

]
ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
ap.add_argument("--tex", default="manuscript/sn-article.tex"); ap.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true"); a = ap.parse_args()
p = Path(a.tex)
if not p.exists(): sys.exit(f"ABORT: {p} tidak ada")
text = open(p, encoding="utf-8", newline="").read(); crlf = "\r\n" in text
if crlf: text = text.replace("\r\n", "\n")
applied, already, skipped, multi = [], [], [], []
for i, sec, old, new in EDITS:
    n_old, n_new = text.count(old), text.count(new)
    if n_old > 1: multi.append(i); continue
    if n_old == 1: text = text.replace(old, new, 1); applied.append(i)
    elif n_new >= 1: already.append(i)
    else: skipped.append(i)
if multi: sys.exit("ABORT, tidak ada yang ditulis: teks lama muncul lebih dari sekali untuk " + ", ".join(multi))
print(f"Diterapkan ({len(applied)}): {', '.join(applied) or '-'}")
print(f"Sudah ada sebelumnya ({len(already)}): {', '.join(already) or '-'}")
print(f"DILEWATI, teks lama tidak cocok ({len(skipped)}): {', '.join(skipped) or '-'}")
if a.dry_run or not applied: print("(dry-run atau tidak ada perubahan; tidak ada yang ditulis)"); sys.exit(0)
out = text.replace("\n", "\r\n") if crlf else text
shutil.copy(p, str(p) + ".pre-prose1.bak"); open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="").write(out)
print("OK: berkas ditulis" + ("; peringatan: ada suntingan dilewati, unggah sn-article.tex terbaru agar saya sesuaikan" if skipped else ""))
